From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Tue, 03 Nov 2026 00:42:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: widen ddsls watch runtime for slow CI hosts

run_ddsls_watchmode launches `ddsls --watch --runtime <N>` as a background
subprocess and starts the test's own async body ~0.3s later; the subprocess
polls the DDS waitset until its own clock reaches N seconds, then writes its
final snapshot (to stdout or --filename) and exits. The N budget leaves
essentially no slack for the SPDP/SEDP discovery and dispose/qos-change
propagation the test depends on to land before that deadline:

- test_ddsls_write_disposed_data_to_file's body sleeps 1s, disposes every
  entity, sleeps 1s more, i.e. ~2.3s of wall clock against runtime=2 -
  dispose already happens near or past the subprocess's own cutoff, so the
  final JSON snapshot can be written before the Disposed transition is
  observed:
      assert dw_guid in data["PUBLICATION"]["Disposed"]
      AssertionError: assert '...' in {}
- test_ddsls_qos_change_in_verbose's qos change happens ~1.3s in against
  runtime=5, with the assertion reading the old Qos's str() out of stdout
  that the subprocess only had until t=5 to print.

On this repo's riscv64 runner (slower interpreter/DDS-stack startup than the
x86_64 hardware upstream's own CI sizes these budgets for) both windows are
tight enough to flake: the exact same job rerunning failing tests with
`pytest --lf` reproduced test_ddsls_write_disposed_data_to_file passing and
test_ddsls_qos_change_in_verbose failing again, consistent with a timing race
rather than a deterministic break.

Raise the watcher's `--runtime` budget for the three tests built on this
exact "watch, mutate, assert on the final snapshot" shape
(test_ddsls_write_disposed_data_to_file, test_ddsls_qos_change,
test_ddsls_qos_change_in_verbose) so the subprocess stays alive well past
every test body's own sleeps, on any host speed. The test bodies and
assertions are otherwise untouched: this only gives the DDS discovery and
the watcher more wall-clock room to actually observe the mutation before the
subprocess is told to stop and dump its snapshot.

Follow-up (gotcha 708): the above alone turned green locally but still failed
in CI, because run_ddsls()'s own process.communicate() kill-timeout defaults
to a fixed 10s that run_ddsls_async_watch never overrides - completely
unrelated to the --runtime value handed to the watched subprocess. Raising
test_ddsls_qos_change/test_ddsls_qos_change_in_verbose's runtime to 10 above
pushed the inner watch budget up to exactly that hardcoded outer kill-timeout,
deleting all headroom for the subprocess's own startup (interpreter boot +
cyclonedds import, before --runtime's own clock even starts) and shutdown
(DDS participant teardown, stdout flush, process exit): both tests then hit
`subprocess.TimeoutExpired` at 10s in the next CI run, one with a partial
stdout capture proving the subprocess was still working, not deadlocked.
Thread a `timeout` through to run_ddsls() from run_ddsls_async_watch that is
always `runtime` plus a fixed 20s of headroom, so this harness function can
never again let a bump to some test's runtime collide with its own
kill-timeout.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64 runner is slower than the hardware this fixed budget was sized for; not a defect on upstream's own CI]

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
diff --git a/tests/test_ddsls.py b/tests/test_ddsls.py
--- a/tests/test_ddsls.py
+++ b/tests/test_ddsls.py
@@ -69,7 +69,14 @@ async def run_ddsls_async_watch(args, runtime, runner):
 async def run_ddsls_async_watch(args, runtime, runner):
     loop = asyncio.get_event_loop_policy().get_event_loop()
     with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor() as pool:
-        task = loop.run_in_executor(pool, run_ddsls, ["--watch", "--runtime", str(runtime)] + args)
+        # gotcha 708: run_ddsls()'s own kill-timeout defaults to a fixed 10s unrelated to
+        # the --runtime budget handed to the subprocess below; raising runtime (in the
+        # tests further down) without raising this too leaves zero headroom for the
+        # subprocess's own startup/shutdown. Always give the kill-timeout real headroom
+        # above runtime instead of relying on run_ddsls()'s unrelated default.
+        task = loop.run_in_executor(
+            pool,
+            lambda: run_ddsls(["--watch", "--runtime", str(runtime)] + args, timeout=runtime + 20))
         await asyncio.sleep(0.3)
         test = await runner()
         return (await task), test
@@ -466,7 +473,7 @@ def test_ddsls_write_disposed_data_to_file(tmp_path):
         return disposed_data

     procdata, disposed_data = run_ddsls_watchmode(["--json", "-a", "--filename", str(tmp_path / "test_disposed.json")],
-                                           test_inner, runtime=2)
+                                           test_inner, runtime=8)

     assert procdata['status'] == 0

@@ -522,7 +529,7 @@ def test_ddsls_qos_change():

         return dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos

-    data, (dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos) = run_ddsls_watchmode(["-a"], test_inner, runtime=5)
+    data, (dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos) = run_ddsls_watchmode(["-a"], test_inner, runtime=10)

     assert str(old_qos) in data["stdout"]
     for q in new_qos:
@@ -550,7 +557,7 @@ def test_ddsls_qos_change_in_verbose():

         return dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos

-    data, (dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos) = run_ddsls_watchmode(["-a", "--verbose"], test_inner, runtime=5)
+    data, (dp, tp, dw, old_qos, new_qos) = run_ddsls_watchmode(["-a", "--verbose"], test_inner, runtime=10)

     assert str(old_qos) in data["stdout"]
     for q in new_qos:
